nd F>=950 maximizing with ceiling 1000), one IDENTITY (B=1000), and the humility ceiling — and LAYER 6 NEEDS NO R5-SHAPED RULING OF ITS OWN, because R5 clause 2 is forward-binding in its own text 'because Layer 6 needs it immediately (Brier<=40 and ECE<=30 are both minimizing)' and R5's regularization already named B=1000 an identity since Layer 1. This is not where Layer 6 gets hard, and saying so is half the value of reading ahead. EXACT COMPUTABILITY, confirmed rather than assumed: all three are exact in Fraction under §2.2's float prohibition at §3.4's declared bin structure — Brier's terms have denominator dividing 10^6 and the mean divides 10^6·A; ECE's ten bins are exactly conf//100 on an integer permille with the single closed-last-bin case conf=1000 -> bin 9, a partition with no ambiguous boundary; AUROC's half-integer U over n_pos·n_neg divides 2·n_pos·n_neg. ONE READING TO DECLARE, the Layer-6 analogue of R4 clause 2's footprint: §3.4 defines all three in [0,1] while §5 L6 states the gates as the integers 40/30/900, so permille(x)<=40 and x<=40/1000 differ on (40/1000, 81/2000] — the endpoint exact because §3.5 rounds a half to the EVEN neighbour and 40 is even — with (30/1000, 61/2000] for ECE and [1799/2000, 900/1000) for AUROC; §5.1 L6's own defense sentences ('at or under 0.04', 'to within 3%') favour the exact reading, and it is cheap to settle before a measurement and expensive after. THE COLLISION, and it is NOT the L5 one: §3.0 is CONFIDENCE-BLIND (a wrong answer scores 0 whatever confidence it carries, so a calibrated hedge and a confident lie are the same number) while §3.4 is ABSTENTION-BLIND (an abstention contributes to no calibration quantity at all), so the two ratified laws point the same query in opposite directions — §3.0 pays 100 for converting an error into an abstention and §3.4 needs those errors to SURVIVE AS ANSWERS. Quantified from the ratified numbers alone: F>=950 on an all-answerable battery is 1000w + 900a <= 50, a total error